From: Simon Kammermeier <simonkammermeier1@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Do not memset new areas

add_new_area() zeroes the entire incoming area before inserting it.
The LROS heap is a demand-paged VMA handed to the allocator via
uk_alloc_addmem(), so writing every byte would fault in the whole
(multi-GiB) heap at boot, defeating demand paging. The zeroing is not
needed for correctness: process_area() writes all block headers the
allocator reads, and the contents of free blocks are never inspected.
---
 src/tlsf.c | 1 -
 1 file changed, 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/tlsf.c b/src/tlsf.c
index a05e85f..f04fb67 100644
--- a/src/tlsf.c
+++ b/src/tlsf.c
@@ -504,7 +504,6 @@ size_t add_new_area(void *area, size_t area_size, void *mem_pool)
     area_info_t *ptr, *ptr_prev, *ai;
     bhdr_t *ib0, *b0, *lb0, *ib1, *b1, *lb1, *next_b;

-    memset(area, 0, area_size);
     ptr = tlsf->area_head;
     ptr_prev = 0;
